Public transportation creates jobs in construction, maintenance, and operations, boosting local economies through increased employment and consumer spending.
Accessible public transit ensures that all residents, including low-income and disabled individuals, can access employment and essential services, reducing economic disparities.
Well-connected transit systems increase property values and attract businesses to urban areas, fostering economic development and innovation.
Investing in public transportation reduces traffic congestion and pollution, leading to long-term cost savings for individuals and communities.
Studies show that cities with robust public transit systems experience higher economic growth rates compared to those reliant on private vehicles.